  "content": {
    "body": "Oathkeeper is Pebbles' chat layer. It runs no model itself — it relays\nto the developer's installed harness CLI inside the project directory,\nwrapping every message with the platform contract. Supported harnesses:\nClaude Code (`claude`), Codex (`codex`), OpenCode (`opencode`); a\nskill saved for one harness works when chatting with any of them.\n\n## What every relayed message carries\n\n1. Identity: \"you are the working harness under Oathkeeper; specs are\n   binding instructions, not background reading.\"\n2. Whole-graph scope: .pebbles/ is always in scope; PEBBLES.md is the\n   index; rules/ and architecture/ are enforceable.\n3. FOCUSED CONTEXT: the open pebble (full JSON + file path) and the\n   precisely selected unit (field/item/node) when one is selected.\n4. The INTENT — what to do with the context (see below).\n5. Extra context pebbles the user attached (searchable + add picker).\n6. Attachments: files (in-project ones annotated with their map-file\n   hint; .pebble files flagged read-only) and folders (explore\n   semantics — other projects for cross-repo context, skill folders).\n7. The CLI contract: command cheat sheet, pre-approval rules, the\n   PROJECT MAP STATUS (age + file count, or \"NO MAP YET\").\n8. SPEC RULES: composed live from .pebbles/rules/system/ pebbles —\n   user-tuned wording wins; factory text is the fallback.\n9. Conversation history and resume state (the harness session id is\n   preserved so follow-ups continue the same CLI conversation).\n\n## Intents\n\nIntents are GENERAL (the chat's goal); skills are SPECIFIC (the\nprocedure). There are seven, each owning a \"regular\" skill: discover\n(understand the ticket/context + spec readiness, read-only — run it first) · extract\n(reverse mode; three depths — shallow/extract/deep) · verify · fix ·\nplan · impact · investigate. Default is NONE — the user picks one. A\nselected intent RUNS its regular skill when no /skill is invoked, and is\npurely additive when one is. The intent also pulls its activity rules,\ne.g. verify = \"compare reality to claim, set truthful statuses, record a\nfinding either way.\"\n\n## Permission enforcement (the approver, not the harness)\n\n- Direct Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit on any .pebbles/ path is\n  DENIED in EVERY mode, bypass included. Reads are free.\n- Pure pebbles-cli Bash calls are AUTO-ALLOWED in every mode. \"Pure\" is\n  parsed, not pattern-matched: quote-aware tokenizer, VAR=value\n  assignment lines resolved, statements split on newlines/';'. Any\n  other program, && or | chaining, redirects, backticks, $(…), or\n  execution-hijacking env names (PATH, LD_*, IFS…) falls through to a\n  manual prompt.\n- The session permission mode (default = no flag, plan, acceptEdits,\n  bypassPermissions)\n  is enforced by Pebbles' own approver, so it sticks even when org\n  policy disables CLI-level bypass.\n- Denied rules are offered back as one-click session grants\n  (--allowedTools); grants stay strictly per-session.\n\n## Run lifecycle and the timeline\n\nEvery run is journaled (crash recovery: orphaned processes are killed\non next launch and the session gets a resumable \"interrupted\" marker).\nA run renders as an APPEND-ONLY timeline: markdown text blocks, tool\ncalls as terminal boxes ($ command, live status, captured output ≤1600\nchars), thinking markers, permission decisions, notes. The same events\npersist on the saved message — history shows exactly what happened.\nRuns can be stopped; tools still running at run end are marked failed.\n\n## Sessions and settings\n\nChats are per-developer data in the OS app-data dir (never the repo),\nkeyed per project. Each session OWNS its settings (model/effort/mode);\nevery settings touch becomes the per-harness GLOBAL default seeding new\nsessions; existing sessions are never rewritten by the global.",
    "kind": "text"
  },
